@meta
id: 14-06
title: From here to the end of the file
chapter: 14
type: lesson
skills: sub-range
par: 53
minutes: 6
vscode: scroll to the block, shift-click the end, Ctrl-H in selection
open: refund.test.ts

@brief
The second `describe` block tests the v2 refunds API, and v2 is served
under `/v2`. Every request path in that block, `post` and `get` alike,
needs the prefix: `'/refunds'` becomes `'/v2/refunds'`, and the query
string version does too. The v1 block above it keeps its paths.

Counting line numbers would work, until someone adds a test. A range
that starts at the cursor and runs to the end of the file does not
care how long the file is. Get to the v2 block by search and let the
range take it from there.

The pattern contains a slash, and so does the replacement. Escaping
each one is legal and ugly; there is a cleaner way.

@keys
| `:.,$s/old/new/` `Enter` | from the current line to the last line |
| `:.,+3s/old/new/` `Enter` | this line and the three below it |
| `.` and `$` | as line addresses: the cursor line, the last line |
| `:s#a/b#c/d#` `Enter` | any punctuation can be the delimiter, so `/` needs no escaping |

@hints
Search to the v2 `describe` line first. Then the range is "from here
down", which is two addresses joined by a comma.
--
`.` is the current line and `$` is the last line, so `.,$` is
everything from the cursor down. And since the paths have slashes in
them, pick `#` as the separator for this one command.
--
`/refunds v2` `Enter`, then `:.,$s#'/refunds#'/v2/refunds#` and Enter.
Leaving the closing quote out of the pattern lets it match both the
plain path and the one with a query string.

@solution
    /refunds v2<Enter>  :.,$s#'/refunds#'/v2/refunds#<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Forty-six keystrokes for four changes in the right block and none in
the wrong one.

Two things happened there. The range `.,$` is the workhorse for "from
this point down": `.` is wherever the cursor is, `$` is the last line,
and the search put the cursor exactly where the block starts. Its
cousins are `.,+3` for "here and the next three" and `.,.+3` which is
the same thing spelled out, and `1,.` for "from the top to here". Any
address that works alone works on either side of the comma.

The other is the delimiter. Vim only asks that the separator be a
character that is not in the pattern, so when the text is full of
slashes you use `#`, or `|`, or `,`. Reading `s#'/refunds#'/v2/refunds#`
is easier than reading `s/'\/refunds/'\/v2\/refunds/`, and easier to
type without a mistake, which matters more.

@start refund.test.ts
import { request } from './helpers';

describe('refunds v1', () => {
  it('creates a refund', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_8101' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(201);
  });

  it('rejects an unknown order', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_0000' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(404);
  });

  it('lists refunds for an order', async () => {
    const response = await request.get('/refunds?orderId=ord_8101');
    expect(response.status).toEqual(200);
  });
});

describe('refunds v2', () => {
  it('creates a refund', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_8101', reason: 'DAMAGED' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(201);
  });

  it('requires a reason', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_8101' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(422);
  });

  it('lists refunds for an order', async () => {
    const response = await request.get('/refunds?orderId=ord_8101');
    expect(response.status).toEqual(200);
  });

  it('returns the refund by id', async () => {
    const response = await request.get('/refunds/rfd_0301');
    expect(response.status).toEqual(200);
  });
});

@expected refund.test.ts
import { request } from './helpers';

describe('refunds v1', () => {
  it('creates a refund', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_8101' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(201);
  });

  it('rejects an unknown order', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_0000' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(404);
  });

  it('lists refunds for an order', async () => {
    const response = await request.get('/refunds?orderId=ord_8101');
    expect(response.status).toEqual(200);
  });
});

describe('refunds v2', () => {
  it('creates a refund', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/v2/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_8101', reason: 'DAMAGED' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(201);
  });

  it('requires a reason', async () => {
    const response = await request.post('/v2/refunds', { orderId: 'ord_8101' });
    expect(response.status).toEqual(422);
  });

  it('lists refunds for an order', async () => {
    const response = await request.get('/v2/refunds?orderId=ord_8101');
    expect(response.status).toEqual(200);
  });

  it('returns the refund by id', async () => {
    const response = await request.get('/v2/refunds/rfd_0301');
    expect(response.status).toEqual(200);
  });
});
